['In C language, to execute a system command and store its output into a file, we can use `system` and `popen` functions respectively. Below are the examples for both cases:

1. Using system function:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    char username[50];
    printf("Enter your username: ");
    scanf("%s", username);  // get the username input from user

    FILE *output;
    output = fopen("user_info.txt", "w"); // open a file to write the output

    if(output == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file!");
        return -1;
    } 

    char command[200];
    sprintf(command, "getent passwd %s", username); // prepare the command

    system(command);  // execute the command and store the output in terminal

    fclose(output);
    return 0;
}
```

Please note that the above code will only display the user information on your terminal. If you want to save this output into a file, use `freopen` function as follows:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    char username[50];
    printf("Enter your username: ");
    scanf("%s", username);  // get the username input from user

    FILE *output;
    output = fopen("user_info.txt", "w"); // open a file to write the output

    if(output == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file!");
        return -1;
    } 

    char command[200];
    sprintf(command, "getent passwd %s", username); // prepare the command

    freopen ("user_info.txt","w",stdout); // redirect stdout to file

    system(command);  // execute the command and store the output in file

    fclose(output);
    return 0;
}
```
2. Using popen function:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    char username[50];
    printf("Enter your username: ");
    scanf("%s", username);  // get the username input from user

    FILE *output;
    output = fopen("user_info.txt", "w"); // open a file to write the output

    if(output == NULL) {
        printf("Error opening file!");
        return -1;
    } 

    char command[200];
    sprintf(command, "getent passwd %s", username); // prepare the command

    FILE *pipe = popen(command, "r"); // execute the command and open a pipe to read its output

    if (pipe == NULL) {
        printf("Failed to run command\n" );
        return -1;
    }

    char buffer[256]; 
    while (fgets(buffer, sizeof(buffer)-1, pipe) != NULL) { // read from the pipe and write into file
      fputs(buffer, output);
    }

    pclose(pipe); // close the pipe
    fclose(output); // close the file

    return 0;
}
```
The above code will execute `getent passwd <username>` command and store its output into a file named "user_info.txt". Please replace "<username>" with your actual username before running this program. 

Please note that you need to have sufficient permissions to run the getent command and write into files in your current directory for these examples to work correctly on your machine. Also, make sure `getent` is installed on your system (it's usually present by default in most Unix-based systems). If it isn't or you need to use a different command, just replace "getent passwd <username>" with your command of choice.
']

['Here is a simple C program that uses system() function to execute the command "cat /etc/passwd | grep <username>". This program assumes that you are running it with root privileges or have sufficient permissions to read /etc/passwd. It also assumes that there is no space in usernames and there is no need for escaping special characters in username.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void execute_command(const char *username) {
    char command[256]; 
    snprintf(command, sizeof(command), "cat /etc/passwd | grep %s", username);

    system(command);
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <username>\n", argv[0]);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    execute_command(argv[1]);

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

Please be aware that executing commands with system() is generally not recommended due to security concerns such as command injection. It's a good practice to validate input or use more secure methods like popen(). But for educational purposes this solution should work fine. Also, the program requires root privileges to read /etc/passwd file on Unix-like systems.
']

