['Here's a basic implementation of your code in C, which takes one command-line argument and checks if it is a valid string before trimming any trailing spaces or newlines from it. 

Please note that error checking for `argv[1]` being NULL or not being exactly 2 elements long (including the program name) was added in this version, to be more robust against incorrect user input.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <ctype.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]){
   // check if exactly one argument is provided and it's not null 
   if (argc != 2 || argv[1] == NULL) {
      printf("Usage: %s <string to trim>\n", argv[0]);
      return 1;
   }

   // check that the argument is a string
   int i = 0;
   while (argv[1][i]) {
       if (!isalpha(argv[1][i])) {
           printf("Argument is not a valid string\n");
           return 2;
       }
       ++i;
   }

   // remove trailing spaces from argv[1]
   int len = strlen(argv[1]);
   while (len > 0 && isspace((unsigned char)argv[1][len - 1])) --len;
   argv[1][len] = '\0';

   // remove newlines from argv[1]
   for (int i = 0; argv[1][i]; ++i) {
       if (argv[1][i] == '\n') {
           argv[1][i] = ' ';
       }
   }

   printf("Trimmed string: %s\n", argv[1]);
   return 0;
}
```

Please note that C does not have a direct method for removing newlines from a string. In this code, any '\n' characters are replaced with a space ' '. If you want to remove them entirely instead, you would need to create a new string without the '\n' characters. 

Also remember to always ensure that your input is properly validated and cleaned up to prevent security issues such as buffer overflows or shell injection attacks.
']

['Here is the C code corresponding to your requirements:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

// Function to trim trailing whitespace from a string
void rtrim(char *str) {
    char* back = str + strlen(str);
    while (isspace((unsigned char)*--back))
        *back = '\0';
}

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {

    // Check if exactly one argument is provided
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Error: Please provide exactly one argument.\n");
        exit(1); 
    }

    // Get the argument from the command line and store it in a variable called input
    char *input = argv[1];

    // Print the argument
    printf("Original Input : %s\n", input);

    // Trim trailing whitespace from the argument
    rtrim(input);

    // Print the argument again after trimming the white spaces
    printf("Input after trimming : %s\n", input);

    exit(0); 
}
```
In this code, we check if exactly one argument is provided by checking `argc`. If not, we print an error message and exit the program with a non-zero status (this could be useful for other programs calling our script). 

We then store the input in the variable `input` using `argv[1]`, which is the first command line argument passed to the program. We print this original string before trimming any whitespace from it. 

The function `rtrim()` takes a string and modifies it by removing trailing whitespaces. After applying this function on our input, we then print the modified input. 

Finally, we exit the program with a zero status to indicate that everything went well.
']

